infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Value shape vs pointer shape: the generics performance asymmetry
  desc Converting interface params to generics wins for value types, ties for pointers — and always costs build time and bytes
  compares
    - label Value-shape instantiation — slices.Sort on []int
      children
        - label Direct, inlinable calls
          desc Dedicated code per shape; no dynamic dispatch in the hot loop
        - label No boxing allocations
          desc The value never heap-escapes into an interface{}
        - label Runtime: tens of % to a few× faster
          desc vs an interface{}-based version — the canonical container/algorithm win
        - label Cost: machine code per shape
          desc Every distinct value shape adds binary size and build time
    - label Pointer-shape instantiation — io.Reader → [R io.Reader]
      children
        - label All pointers share one gcshape
          desc *os.File and *bytes.Reader collapse into a single instantiation
        - label Calls go through the dictionary
          desc Indirect, non-inlinable — an interface call by another name
        - label Runtime: flat
          desc Same dispatch cost as the interface it replaced; benchmarks unchanged
        - label Cost: +12% binary, +~33% build, zero gain
          desc What the modernization sprint measured before reverting 38 of 40
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
